  "description": "Simulate SQL LEFT OUTER JOIN matching all left-table records with corresponding right-table records.\n\nLeft outer joins preserve all rows from the primary left table regardless of whether relational integrity constraints find corresponding secondary records.\n\n### Query Logic & Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{R} = \\text{Users} \\rtimes_{\\text{id}=\\text{user\\_id}} \\text{Orders}\n$$\n\n### Relational Execution Steps\n1. Iterate over each row in left table\n2. Identify all matching rows in right table sharing join key\n3. Emit joined record, preserving left table attributes if no right match exists\n\n### Expected Query Output\nList of joined record dictionaries.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: LEFT JOIN\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRelational databases structure data into normalized tables with strict schemas and foreign-key relationships. SQL allows data engineers and analysts to write declarative queries describing what data is needed, while the database query engine optimizes the underlying execution plan. **LEFT JOIN** combines, aggregates, or transforms records across tables.\n\nLeft outer joins preserve all rows from the primary left table regardless of whether relational integrity constraints find corresponding secondary records. In this problem, the goal is to: simulate sql left outer join matching all left-table records with corresponding right-table records.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{R} = \\text{Users} \\rtimes_{\\text{id}=\\text{user\\_id}} \\text{Orders}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Iterate over each row in left table\n2. **Phase 2**: Identify all matching rows in right table sharing join key\n3. **Phase 3**: Emit joined record, preserving left table attributes if no right match exists\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Join Mechanics**: Inner joins filter for matching records across tables, while outer joins preserve all rows from one table even when no matches exist.\n* **Window Functions**: Functions like `ROW_NUMBER()` and `RANK()` allow computing partitioned running totals and top-K ranks without requiring self-joins.\n* **Index Optimization**: Proper indexing on join keys and filter conditions ensures queries run in milliseconds rather than doing full table scans.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: users = [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"Bob\"}], orders = [{\"order_id\": 101, \"user_id\": 1, \"amount\": 50}], user_key = \"id\", order_key = \"user_id\"\n\nOutput: [{\"id\": 1, \"name\": \"Alice\", \"order_id\": 101, \"user_id\": 1, \"amount\": 50}, {\"id\": 2, \"name\": \"Bob\"}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated sql_left_join under standard test conditions."
